Why This Is Asked
This question tests building a resilient real-time frontend: proper WebSocket lifecycle with dedupe and ordering, offline-first caching via Service Worker, robust fetch error handling, and state-preserving DOM updates. It also probes offline mutation queues and replay semantics across reconnects.

## Key Concepts
- WebSocket lifecycle with exponential backoff on reconnect
- Message deduping and in-order application
- Service Worker caching strategies for REST/data
- Robust fetch retry and error handling with jitter
- IndexedDB-backed offline queue and replay
- Debounced/batched DOM rendering that preserves focus/input state

## Code Example
```javascript
class WSClient {
  constructor(url) {
    this.url = url;
    this.backoff = 1000;
    this.connect();
  }
  connect() {
    this.ws = new WebSocket(this.url);
    this.ws.onopen = () => { this.backoff = 1000; };
    this.ws.onmessage = (ev) => this.handle(JSON.parse(ev.data));
    this.ws.onerror = () => this.reconnect();
    this.ws.onclose = () => this.reconnect();
  }
  reconnect() {
    setTimeout(() => this.connect(), this.backoff);
    this.backoff = Math.min(this.backoff * 2, 30000);
  }
  handle(msg) {
    // dedupe by msgId, apply in-order, enqueue if needed
  }
}
```

